Package: qavector
Version: @VERSION@
Section: web
Priority: optional
Architecture: amd64
Maintainer: @MAINTAINER@
Installed-Size: @INSTALLED_SIZE@
Depends: libc6 (>= 2.35), libgl1, libegl1, libglib2.0-0, libdbus-1-3, libfontconfig1, libfreetype6, libx11-6, libx11-xcb1, libxcb1, libxcb-cursor0, libxcb-icccm4, libxcb-image0, libxcb-keysyms1, libxcb-randr0, libxcb-render-util0, libxcb-render0, libxcb-shape0, libxcb-shm0, libxcb-sync1, libxcb-xfixes0, libxcb-xinerama0, libxcb-xkb1, libxkbcommon0, libxkbcommon-x11-0, libsm6, libice6, libnss3
Suggests: google-chrome-stable
Replaces: chrome-multi-session
Conflicts: chrome-multi-session
Description: Launch one isolated, auto-logged-in Chrome window per test user
 QAVector opens a separate Chrome profile per test user, so every
 user can be signed in at the same time, and drives declarative YAML flows
 against those windows over the Chrome DevTools Protocol.
 .
 Ships as a self-contained application: the Python runtime, PySide6 and
 Playwright are bundled, so nothing has to be installed to run it. Google
 Chrome itself is not bundled and must be installed separately - the app says
 so on startup if it cannot find one.
 .
 This package installs the desktop front-end and the qavector
 command-line launcher. Sessions, credentials and run reports are kept in
 ~/QAVector and are never touched by an upgrade.
 .
 QAVector was called chrome-multi-session before 0.15.0; this package
 replaces that one, and the first start moves its folders to the new names.
